  "body": {
    "id": "thm:symmetric-family",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "If \\(e_x=1/2\\), \\(p_1=(1+c)/2\\), \\(p_0=(1-c)/2\\), and \\(\\kappa=(\\Gamma-1)/(\\Gamma+1)\\), then \\(D_P=\\{\\tau\\in(-1,1):|c-\\tau|\\leq\\kappa(1-|\\tau|)\\}\\) and \\(C_P(\\tau)=(c-\\tau)^2/(1-|\\tau|)\\) on \\(D_P\\). In particular, for \\(c=3/5\\) and \\(\\Gamma=4\\), \\(D_P=[0,3/4]\\) and \\(C_P(0)=9/25\\).",
    "free_symbols": [
      "e_x",
      "p_1",
      "p_0",
      "c",
      "\\Gamma",
      "\\kappa",
      "D_P",
      "\\tau",
      "C_P"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "thm:binary-phase-catalog"
    ],
    "justification": "The family supplies a closed-form stress test for the full phase catalog and the infinite-sign-reversal boundary.",
    "gap": "Published average-case bounds do not give this joint-response ATE profile or its truncated domain.",
    "consumer": "The formula yields exact sensitivity intervals and sign-breakdown budgets without optimization.",
    "proof_tex": "First let \\(0<\\tau<1\\). Under the symmetric law,\n\\[\nA=B=\\frac{1-c}{4},\\qquad D_0=\\frac12. \\tag{33}\n\\]\nIn \\(\\mathrm{thm{:}binary\\mbox{-}phase\\mbox{-}catalog}\\), put \\(v_\\star=(1-\\tau)/2\\). The propensities in (21) become\n\\[\nq_{00}=\\frac{1-c}{2(1-\\tau)},\\qquad q_{01}=\\frac12,\\qquad q_{11}=1-q_{00}. \\tag{34}\n\\]\nAt \\(v_\\star\\), the first two terms of \\(H_\\tau\\) cancel, and \\(2(v_\\star+\\tau-D_0)/\\tau-1=0\\). Hence \\(H_\\tau(v_\\star)=0\\), and strict increase makes \\(v_\\star\\) the unique unconstrained minimizer.\n\nFor \\(e_x=1/2\\),\n\\[\n\\ell_x=\\frac{1-\\kappa}{2},\\qquad u_x=\\frac{1+\\kappa}{2}. \\tag{35}\n\\]\nThe middle propensity in (34) is feasible, and the other two are feasible exactly when\n\\[\n\\left|q_{00}-\\frac12\\right|\n=\\frac{|c-\\tau|}{2(1-\\tau)}\\leq\\frac{\\kappa}{2}, \\tag{36}\n\\]\nor \\(|c-\\tau|\\leq\\kappa(1-\\tau)\\). Substituting (33) into all six scalar bounds shows their intersection is nonempty under exactly this inequality, so (36) is exact fiber feasibility.\n\nAt \\(v_\\star\\), the \\(01\\) assignment deviation is zero and the concordant deviations are opposite. Substitution in \\(F_\\tau\\) yields\n\\[\nC_P(\\tau)=\\frac{(c-\\tau)^2}{1-\\tau}. \\tag{37}\n\\]\nFor \\(\\tau<0\\), complementing both outcomes sends \\((c,\\tau)\\) to \\((-c,-\\tau)\\) and preserves cost. At \\(\\tau=0\\), use \\(\\mathrm{prop{:}null\\mbox{-}correlation\\mbox{-}cost}\\). Since \\(c\\in(-1,1)\\), neither \\(\\tau=1\\) nor \\(\\tau=-1\\) matches the margins. Therefore\n\\[\nD_P=\\{\\tau\\in(-1,1):|c-\\tau|\\leq\\kappa(1-|\\tau|)\\},\\qquad\nC_P(\\tau)=\\frac{(c-\\tau)^2}{1-|\\tau|}. \\tag{38}\n\\]\nFor \\(c=3/5\\) and \\(\\Gamma=4\\), \\(\\kappa=3/5\\). On \\(\\tau<0\\), feasibility would imply \\(3/5-\\tau\\leq(3/5)(1+\\tau)\\), hence \\(\\tau\\geq0\\), a contradiction. On \\(\\tau\\geq0\\), (38) holds exactly for \\(0\\leq\\tau\\leq3/4\\). Thus \\(D_P=[0,3/4]\\) and \\(C_P(0)=9/25\\).",
